{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Judges, etc.</b> Scripture juxtaposed this commandment to the commandment of ״three times״ etc. preceding it, to awaken [us] that even though you are commanded to go up three times a year to see the face of HaShem, and there stands the fixed Great Sanhedrin — even so, you must appoint judges, etc., and not rely solely on the court that is before HaShem in the Chamber of Hewn Stone. And the reason is so that the judgment not suffer delay until one goes up to the Place." "And the reason for the officers: they said in the Pesikta: Rabbi Elazar ben Shammua says, if there are officers, there are judges; if there are no officers, there are no judges — end quote. The words prove that if there are no officers there is no obligation of the commandment of judges. From this point onward, whenever Israel knows that the people do not heed the judges and the judges cannot compel them — there is no obligation to appoint judges; and wherever the people heed their judges without officers, they are obligated to appoint judges. And we call such a judge both judge and officer." "<b>You shall give for yourself.</b> Meaning, that those who appoint them of their own accord should not say in their minds that the rod of authority of the judge and officer should not have dominion over them, since they are the ones who appoint him. Rather, they should appoint them to judge and to rule over them. And that is what it says, <b>you shall give for yourself</b> — meaning, over yourself." "<b>In all your gates.</b> The reason it says <b>בְּכָל (in all)</b> is that one must appoint [judges] in every single city and in every single province in the land of Israel. And in the baraita (Yalkut Shimoni) they said, and this is their language: ״בְּכָל שְׁעָרֶיךָ״ — the verse comes to juxtapose the minor Sanhedrin to the Great Sanhedrin: just as the Great [Sanhedrin] judges and executes, so too the minor [Sanhedrin] judges and executes — end quote. And it appears that their derashah derives from the word ״בְּכָל,״ for the verse included all the Sanhedrins together to say that they are equal in one power." "And its saying <b>which HaShem your Elohim</b> gives you — they of blessed memory said (Makkot 7a) that this comes to say that the obligation mentioned in the matter applies only in the land which HaShem your Elohim gives you, and not outside the land." "And the verse's saying <b>for your tribes</b> — our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sifrei) explained it as referring back to what precedes, and it is difficult: why did it not write it in its place in this manner: ״תִּתֶּן לְךָ לִשְׁבָטֶיךָ בְּכָל שְׁעָרֶיךָ אֲשֶׁר״ etc.? Perhaps it intended to attach it to the statement ״וְשָׁפְטוּ אֶת הָעָם מִשְׁפַּט צֶדֶק״ to indicate that this is the reason He commands to appoint judges in every city, and in a city that has two tribes, two judges, so that they may judge with just judgment. And it was precise in saying <b>et the people</b>, meaning that there be no distinction among them and they be judged one against the other on equal footing, since each tribe is judged before its own court. And through this we arrive at a satisfactory understanding of the statement <b>and they shall judge</b>, for if it came to warn the judges it should have spoken in the second person in the order it spoke in the adjacent passage: ״לֹא תַטֶּה לֹא תַכִּיר״ etc. And Rashi of blessed memory explained that it is a warning to the appointers to appoint the righteous, and according to his words it should have said in this manner: ״judges who judge with righteousness etc. תִּתֶּן לְךָ״ etc., and according to our approach it comes out correctly." "<b>And I saw</b> that the Rambam of blessed memory (Hilkhot Sanhedrin, chapter 1) omitted this ruling that each and every tribe requires its own, and wrote only that each and every city requires its own. Perhaps the Rambam holds that the intent of our Rabbis of blessed memory who expounded ״for your tribes״ — that a court is needed for every tribe — is prior to their expounding that every city requires one, but once every city is required there is no longer a need to appoint for every tribe. And this is the language of the Baraita in Sanhedrin (16b): The Rabbis taught — from where do we know that we establish judges for Israel? Scripture says ״שֹׁפְטִים תִּתֶּן״ etc. For every tribe — from where? Scripture says ״שֹׁפְטִים לִשְׁבָטֶיךָ״. For every city — from where? Scripture says ״שֹׁפְטִים לִשְׁעָרֶיךָ״ — end of quote. And it is difficult: why did the Tanna expound the word ״for your tribes,״ which is at the end of the verse, before the word ״in all your gates,״ which precedes it — for the Tanna should have prefaced the exposition of ״in all your gates״? Rather, this tells us that the obligation of ״for your tribes״ applies only prior to the obligation of all your gates, but after He commanded ״in all your gates״ there is no place to require it for the tribes. And therefore the Rambam, who wrote the obligation for every city, has no place to say for every tribe. And according to this approach, the Tanna's exposition proceeds in this manner: Scripture obligates you to appoint judges for every tribe, whether it be in ten cities or in one city, and then it went back and said that if there are many cities, you shall appoint for every city." "<b>And if</b> you say: if He appoints for every city, why was it necessary to say ״for your tribes״? Because without ״for your tribes״ I would not have interpreted ״in all your gates״ as every single city, for I would have said it means your gates of the collective of Israel, whether in the land of Israel or across the Jordan, not those of every single city in the land. And after it said ״for your tribes,״ which means for every tribe, we became obligated to interpret ״in all your gates״ as every city. And therefore when the Rambam ruled for every city, there is no place to say ״for your tribes,״ for then you would interpret his words according to the words of the Tosafot (Sanhedrin 56a) that if there are two tribes in a city, two courts are required." "<b>Just judgment.</b> And it did not say ״in righteousness״ as it says (Leviticus 19:15) ״בְּצֶדֶק תִּשְׁפֹּט עֲמִיתֶךָ״ — here it intimated a great lesson for one who wishes to judge in righteousness, and it is this: when the judge wishes to determine the law of the people who are parties to the dispute, it should be in this manner — that he remove from before his eyes the two litigants and not clothe himself with the considerations of the legal give-and-take that he will apply to the people who are parties, saying ״according to this, so-and-so is liable, and according to this it would be the opposite.״ Rather, he should conduct his analysis and examine the sides of the law as though there is no person who needs this ruling right now, but only to determine the law and know the laws of the Torah. And after he determines it in this manner, whatever his analysis yields — with that he will judge the litigants, acquitting the one to be acquitted and obligating the one to be obligated. And this is what it means by <b>and they shall judge the people with judgment</b> — meaning, it is already a judgment in and of itself; this will constitute righteousness. But without this, when he weighs in his mind at the time he frames and constructs the ruling for the litigants, in any case one will be more favored in his mind toward him, and the judgment will not come out as righteousness — and especially in judgments that depend on the weighing of reason, with a slight tilt his mind will incline." "<b>And by way of</b> allusion, it alludes to what is said (Psalms 96:13) ״יִשְׁפֹּט תֵּבֵל בְּצֶדֶק,״ and the men of truth said (Zohar 3:198a) that the supernal court is called tzedek, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Beitzah 16a) that on Rosh HaShanah a person's provisions are fixed, etc. Now, when the discrepancy falls upon property in a dispute between one person and another and they stand before the judge, if he judges according to the Torah that HaShem commanded, acquitting the one who should be acquitted, etc. — behold, they are judging the very judgment itself that the supernal court called tzedek has judged. And this is the meaning of <b>and they shall judge the people with just judgment</b> — meaning, the supernal court called tzedek has already judged it."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:14.273915946Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}